gbgk(){return"On the beat, or off?"},
gbgj(){return"Usually the notes land right ON the beats \u2014 count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square."},
gbgi(){return'Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the "and" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it \u2014 thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.'},
gbgG(){return"Two or three in a beat"},
gbgE(){return'Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: "1-and". Two eighth notes.'},
gbgF(){return'A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: "trip-o-let". It gets a little 3 above it.'},
gbfB(){return"Decorating a note"},
gbfC(){return"Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it."},
gbfD(){return"A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back."},
ga3u(){return"Label the Form"},
garo(){return"Hear the sections; name the shape (ABA\u2026)"},
gb1k(){return"What is the form? (same colour = same tune)"},
gb1j(){return"Listen"},
gbeF(){return"The shape of a piece"},
gbeE(){return"Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune \u2014 call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again."},
gbeD(){return"A different tune is a new letter \u2014 section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!"},
ga3W(){return"Which Mode?"},
garQ(){return"Major, minor or Dorian?"},
gb9W(){return"Listen! Which mode is it?"},
ga0w(){return"Major"},
ga0x(){return"Minor"},
gb9V(){return"Dorian"},
gbfx(){return"Three colours of scale"},
gbfv(){return"A major scale sounds bright and happy. Listen to it climb."},
gbfw(){return"A minor scale sounds darker \u2014 its 3rd, 6th and 7th steps sit a little lower."},
gbfu(){return"Dorian is like minor, but its 6th step is raised \u2014 so it sounds minor with a brighter twist. That one note is the whole secret!"},
ganB(){return"Textbook"},
gblK(){return"Read"},
gblH(){return"Work through music from the very start. Each topic has a short lesson (see it, hear it) and games to practise it."},
